title: How We Use GitHub Actions
page: github-actions.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: GitHub Actions runs a script when something happens in your repo, and the fastest way to learn it is to read someone else's working YAML.

== Learn Actions by reading working YAML
layout: text
section: top
GitHub Actions runs a script when something happens in your repo: a push, a pull request, a scheduled time or a button click. Everything else is configuration. Each concept on the page points at a working file in CSOH's repo.
notes:
The page calls GitHub Actions the friendliest CI/CD platform you'll meet. It runs a script when something happens in your repo: a push, a pull request, a scheduled time, or a button click. Everything else is configuration.

The docs are good but vast. The page's view is that the fastest way to get Actions is to read someone else's working YAML and trace what each line does, so every concept comes with a pointer to a line in a file in CSOH's own repo.

What you'll need: a GitHub repository, comfort with git basics, and willingness to read shell. No prior CI/CD experience is required.

== The absolute minimum workflow
layout: code
section: what-is-it
A file under .github/workflows/ names the workflow, lists its triggering events under on, and defines jobs made of steps. When an event fires, GitHub spins up a fresh VM (the runner), runs your steps and tears it down.
```yaml
name: Hello
on:
  push:
jobs:
  greet:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: echo "Hi, $GITHUB_ACTOR pushed to $GITHUB_REF"
```
notes:
GitHub Actions is GitHub's built-in automation runner. You drop a YAML file under .github/workflows/, and GitHub watches your repository for the events you list: a push to main, a new pull request, a cron schedule, a manual button click. When a matching event fires, GitHub spins up a fresh virtual machine, the runner, runs the steps you wrote, and tears it down.

Every workflow file boils down to four parts. name is what to call the workflow in the UI. on is what events trigger it. jobs is one or more named bundles of steps. steps is what to do: run a script, use a published Action, or set an output.

Drop this file at .github/workflows/hello.yml and every push to your repo produces a 5-second run that prints your username. From there the surface area expands fast, but the shape stays the same.

== What's good, and where it isn't
layout: compare
section: why-cool
col: What's good | Zero infrastructure: GitHub gives you fresh VMs | Free for public repos, generous for private | 20,000+ Marketplace Actions, most 2-3 lines to use | Free schedules, and every run logged and downloadable
col: Where it isn't | YAML breaks easily; most mistakes show only after a push | Hard to test locally: the real test is push and watch | Private repos' free tier is metered; macOS and Windows cost more | Some sharp footguns, like pull_request_target with secrets
Actions comment on PRs and set commit statuses with no external bot infrastructure.
notes:
On the good side: there is no Jenkins server, no agents to patch and no SSH keys to rotate. You write YAML and GitHub gives you fresh VMs. Public repos get unlimited Linux minutes, and private repos get a meaningful free tier for small teams. The Marketplace has 20,000+ pre-built Actions for common tasks such as checkout, build, deploy, signing artifacts and posting Slack messages, and most are 2-3 lines to use. Schedules are cron on the internet, free: CSOH uses them to refresh news every 3 hours and run weekly link checks. Every run, every step and every line of output is logged and downloadable.

On the other side: YAML is whitespace-sensitive and gives no compile-time error for most mistakes, so you push, you wait, and you find out it didn't fire. Tools like act exist but only approximate the real environment. A typo in an if: condition is a 30-second wait per attempt to verify. Heavy CI use on private repos can rack up minutes.

The page calls pull_request_target plus secrets the canonical way to leak credentials to a malicious PR. The safer patterns come later in this deck.

== Anatomy of a workflow
layout: figure
section: anatomy
figure: wf-fig-title
An event triggers the workflow. It defines one or more jobs, each made of steps that run on a runner.
notes:
A workflow is triggered by an event and defines one or more jobs, each composed of steps that run on a runner.

Read the picture from the trigger to the workflow, then to parallel jobs on ephemeral runners, and finally to artifacts uploaded back. The needs graph is how you sequence work: in this picture, deploy depends on build and validate.

The next slides follow validate-html.yml, which is short and shows most of the moving parts. The file carries matching §1 to §4 markers in its comments, so you can search it for §3 to jump straight to that part.

== §1 Triggers: when the workflow runs
layout: code
section: anatomy | workflows-tour
This trigger block runs the workflow when a PR touches any HTML file, every Monday at 07:00 UTC, or on a Run workflow click. Path filters keep CI fast; validate-html.yml, a required check, filters in a step instead.
```yaml
on:
  pull_request:
    paths:
      - '**.html'
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 7 * * 1'
  workflow_dispatch:
```
notes:
This trigger block runs the workflow in three situations: when a pull request touches any HTML file, every Monday at 07:00 UTC from the cron schedule, or whenever someone clicks Run workflow, which is what workflow_dispatch adds.

The page calls the paths filter the secret to a fast CI: if you only validate HTML, don't run on CSS-only PRs.

There is one exception, and it comes from the tour of the same workflow later on. validate-html.yml is a required status check on main, so its pull_request trigger carries no paths: filter, and the filtering happens in a step instead. One of the closing questions covers why.

== §2 Permissions govern only the ambient token
layout: code
section: anatomy
Pin every workflow to the minimum; this one reads the code and posts PR comments, nothing else. The block governs only the ambient GITHUB_TOKEN, so a job whose writes use their own token should stay read-only here.
```yaml
permissions:
  contents: read
  pull-requests: write
```
notes:
Default GitHub Actions permissions are broader than they need to be. CSOH pins every workflow to the minimum. If a malicious dependency ever lands in one of its Actions, it can't push to the repo, because the workflow never gave it that scope.

The subtlety: permissions: governs the ambient GITHUB_TOKEN and nothing else. It does not constrain any other credential your steps carry. So the question to ask is what the job does with the token GitHub injected for free.

normalize-urls.yml is the worked example. It pushes a branch and opens a pull request every month, so contents: write plus pull-requests: write looks obviously correct. But both writes are done by a separate GitHub App installation token, minted in the job and passed explicitly to actions/checkout and to peter-evans/create-pull-request. No step in that job uses GITHUB_TOKEN at all, so its block is contents: read alone. Nothing about the job's behavior changed, and the ambient credential can no longer write anything.

You can audit for this in about a minute: grep your workflows for token:, and for every step handed an explicit one, ask whether the permissions: block still grants that same power to a credential nobody is using.

== §4 Pin to a SHA, and name every step
layout: code
section: anatomy
The SHA is what runs; the trailing comment is the version for humans. Tags can be moved silently, SHAs cannot. A step's name is what the run UI shows.
```yaml
jobs:
  validate-html:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@de0fac2e4500dabe0009e67214ff5f5447ce83dd  # v6.0.2
      - name: Validate HTML5
        uses: Cyb3r-Jak3/html5validator-action@443b108eb8e134b63a1f8a8ba0c942d552608ed7  # master 2025-09-19
```
notes:
Two habits are visible in this block.

First, pin Actions to a full SHA, not a tag. The trailing comment is the version for humans; the SHA is what runs. Tags can be moved silently, and SHAs cannot. In CSOH's repo, Dependabot bumps these pins.

Second, name every step. name: is what shows up in the run UI. Skip it and your run log fills with lines like Run actions/checkout that nobody can grep.

== Shift security left, and keep the late checks
layout: figure
section: anatomy
figure: sl-title
Six SDLC stages, from Plan to Monitor, with the security controls that fit each one. Earlier in the pipeline is cheaper to fix.
notes:
This is a DevSecOps shift-left timeline: six SDLC stages from Plan to Monitor, with the security controls that fit at each stage. Its message is that earlier in the pipeline is cheaper to fix.

Every control should run as early in the pipeline as it can, but the late-stage controls don't go away: they catch what slipped through.

== CSOH's deploy pipeline, end to end
layout: figure
section: anatomy
figure: orch-title
caption: This pipeline replaces a traditional CI server.
notes:
This is CSOH's GitHub Actions deploy pipeline. The flow runs end to end from a developer's git push, through path filtering, concurrency locking, sequential build steps and validation, to a build-once publish to three clouds, AWS, GCP and Azure, over keyless OIDC.

The page says this pipeline replaces a traditional CI server.

== deploy.yml: build once, publish to three clouds
layout: table
section: workflows-tour
row: Job | What it does
row: build | Search index and a staged dist/; holds no cloud credential
row: publish-aws | S3 sync and CloudFront invalidation
row: publish-azure | Blob sync to the $web static-website container
row: publish-gcp | Container build, Trivy scan, SHA tag push, Cloud Run deploy by digest
row: The last job | Re-derives SRI hashes from the edge; checks live headers against the Cloudflare Terraform
Every cloud authenticates with keyless OIDC, so no cloud secret is stored.
notes:
deploy.yml runs on every push to main that touches site or Docker files. It builds the site once, the search index plus a staged dist/ of the public file set, then fans out to three parallel publish jobs, one per cloud origin.

AWS gets an S3 sync and a CloudFront invalidation. Azure gets a Blob sync to the $web static-website container. GCP takes the container path: build, a Trivy scan failing on HIGH or CRITICAL CVEs, push a SHA tag to Artifact Registry, and deploy a Cloud Run revision by digest.

Every cloud authenticates with keyless OIDC: GCP Workload Identity Federation, an AWS IAM role and an Azure Entra federated credential. So there is no stored service-account key or cloud secret for any of them.

The last job re-derives every versioned asset's SRI hash from what the edge serves, and checks the live security headers against the ones declared in the repo's Cloudflare Terraform. That covers the edge only: the CloudFront policy and the nginx config are not read by CI. A control that quietly stopped applying fails the deploy.

The full architecture, three active/active origins behind a single Cloudflare edge, is written up on the cloud deployment page.

== Cloud trust pins the environment subject
layout: compare
section: workflows-tour
col: Trust the repository claim | Any workflow in the repo can mint deploy credentials | On any branch | Including scheduled jobs that read untrusted web pages
col: Trust the full subject | All three clouds trust the subject naming environment:production | Only workflows declaring environment: production authenticate | The environment's branch rule limits it to main
GCP also accepts environment:qa, which reaches only a separate QA deployer. The branch rule lives in one place, not restated in three cloud dialects that can drift.
notes:
Every publish job is gated by GitHub's environment: production, so only commits on main can deploy. The four jobs that reach a cloud, publish-aws, publish-azure, publish-gcp and purge-cloudflare, declare it. build does not, because it only stages files into an artifact and holds no cloud credential to gate.

The gate is not just a GitHub-side convention. All three clouds pin their trust to the same subject string, repo:<owner>/<repo>:environment:production. The AWS role's trust policy compares it with StringEquals, the Azure federated credential declares it as its subject, and the GCP Workload Identity pool asserts it in both its attribute condition and the identity it grants impersonation to. A workflow that does not declare environment: production cannot authenticate to any of the three. The one exception is environment: qa, which reaches only the QA deployer on GCP.

Trusting the repository claim alone is the easy version, and the page calls it much weaker than it looks: it would let any workflow in the repo, on any branch, including scheduled jobs that read untrusted web pages, mint deploy credentials.

The environment carries its own deployment-branch rule limiting it to main, so the branch restriction follows transitively instead of being restated in three different cloud dialects that can drift apart.

== The rest of the tour
layout: table
section: workflows-tour
row: Workflow | Problem it solves | Read it for
row: site-update-deploy.yml | Housekeeping, committed back to main | Step and job outputs, workflow_dispatch
row: update-news.yml | News refresh every 3 hours | Cron, auto-PR, the two-PAT pattern
row: normalize-urls.yml | Monthly link cleanup | Auto-PR with a mandatory human gate
row: lint.yml | Workflow, Python and YAML lint | Parallel jobs, SHA-pinned actions
row: check-broken-links.yml | External link rot | A one-writer cache, fail: false
row: check-url-safety.yml | Malware and phishing URLs | Hard fail plus a summary comment
notes:
Each workflow solves a single, named problem, and every file is heavily commented for new readers.

site-update-deploy.yml runs on every push to main that touches site files. It walks through housekeeping steps such as SRI hashes, URL normalization, sitemap dates and preview screenshots, committing each back to main if anything changed. Those commits are marked to skip CI so they can't loop, and deploy.yml regenerates most of the same files in its own build. Preview screenshots take too long for that, so when housekeeping pushes new ones, a second job with only the permission to start workflows dispatches deploy.yml.

update-news.yml refreshes news.html from RSS and Atom feeds every 3 hours, opens a PR, auto-approves it using a separate bot account so nobody approves their own PR, and auto-merges if only news files were touched.

normalize-urls.yml strips tracking parameters and resolves redirects on every link once a month, then opens a PR. It is auto-approved but not auto-merged, because cross-domain redirects deserve eyes.

lint.yml runs three parallel jobs on every push and PR: actionlint for workflow YAML, with bundled shellcheck on inline run: blocks, ruff for the Python housekeeping scripts, and yamllint for structural YAML sanity.

check-broken-links.yml crawls every link with lychee, weekly and per PR. The weekly crawl checks everything and caches the links that worked, and PR runs request only links that are new or failing. It is a non-blocking check, because link rot is everywhere and shouldn't gate merges.

check-url-safety.yml validates every external URL against safety lists on PRs and weekly, hard fails if anything unsafe is found, and posts a summary comment.

== update-resources.yml: an agent on a short leash
layout: checklist
section: workflows-tour
check: No shell in the tool allowlist, only in-process tools
check: No interpreter: Bash(python3:*) matches python3 -c and anything after it
check: No command that takes a path: grep . /proc/self/environ reads secrets
check: Mint the repo-write token after the model step
check: Set persist-credentials: false so no token lands on disk
An agent researches the open web and opens a PR. Treat the runner's filesystem and environment as readable by that input, and keep credentials elsewhere.
notes:
Every Monday, claude-code-action researches candidate entries for resources.html and opens a PR. The step fetches pages from the open web, so it processes input that nobody in the repo controls. Four properties do most of the work.

The tool allowlist contains no shell: it is exactly Read, Edit, Glob, Grep, WebSearch and WebFetch. Bash(python3:*) reads as "it may run Python", but it matches python3 -c with anything, which is arbitrary code execution and voids every other restriction. The same trapdoor sits in node, perl, sh and bash. Bash(grep:*) and Bash(wc:*) had to go too: grep takes a path, so grep . /proc/self/environ hands back the step's own environment, secrets included. An allowlist entry for a command that accepts a path is a file-read capability wearing a narrow name. If a job needs Python, check the script into the repo and allowlist that exact path.

The csoh-ci App token that can write to the repo is minted in a step after the Claude Code step, right before the create-PR step that uses it. During research, the environment holds the Claude OAuth token, which buys model usage and grants nothing in the repo or any cloud. The page states the residual risk: Read still accepts absolute paths and WebFetch is unqualified, so a prompt injection can still reach that OAuth token. Moving the mint back to the top of the job would silently undo the control, so the workflow carries a comment saying so.

By default, checkout writes its token into .git/config for the rest of the job. The create-PR step is passed the App token explicitly, so the job sets persist-credentials: false.

== security-impact-review.yml: review before anything runs
layout: table
section: workflows-tour
row: Problem | How the workflow handles it
row: No "run this first" key | A repo setting holds external PRs for approval
row: That hold blocks the review too | pull_request_target is exempt
row: Secrets reach a run on an attacker's PR | Never check out the PR head
row: The PR is hostile input | Read it as data; run none of it
row: The comment needs write access | A second job posts; analysis can't write
PRs not from the repo owner or the csoh-ci App are reviewed before the other PR workflows may start.
notes:
Workflows matching an event all start at once, there is no "run this one first" key, and needs: only orders jobs within a single file. The ordering comes from a repository setting: "Require approval for all external contributors" holds every workflow on an external PR until a maintainer clicks Approve and run. That would hold the reviewing workflow too, which is where pull_request_target earns its place: it runs in the base repository's context, from the base branch's copy of the file, so it is exempt from the approval gate and starts immediately.

That exemption is why the page calls pull_request_target the most-abused trigger in Actions. It hands a write-capable token and repository secrets to a run whose subject is attacker-controlled, and the catastrophe is one line long: check out the PR head, then execute anything from it, such as a build, a test, a dependency install, or a linter that loads a repo-local plugin. The checkout takes the base ref, which the trigger supplies by default. Adding a ref: pointing at the PR's head SHA is the one edit that would break it open, and the file says so.

The job fetches a .diff and a .json of metadata through the API and hands them to a checked-in Python script. Nothing from the fork is executed, sourced, imported or installed.

The analysis job holds contents: read and pull-requests: read. Posting the comment is a second job with pull-requests: write, and it only handles a report the repository's own script produced. The csoh-ci App token is never minted in either.

== Two layers, and only one votes
layout: compare
section: workflows-tour
col: tools/pr_security_triage.py | Fixed rules on the diff | Sets the exit code, which is the verdict | Flags tag-pinned actions, credential-shaped strings, invisible Unicode
col: Claude Code step | A narrative review of what merging would do | Reads prose the contributor wrote | Cannot change the verdict
A prompt injection saying "report this as safe" addresses the layer with no vote. If the diff fetch returns zero bytes, the job fails and says it reviewed nothing.
notes:
tools/pr_security_triage.py applies fixed rules to the diff and sets the exit code. It flags CI edits, actions pinned to a tag instead of a commit, credential-shaped strings, symlinks, invisible Unicode, and edits to the publish and header boundary. A Claude Code step then writes a narrative "what would merging this do" review, and cannot change the verdict.

The page calls that asymmetry the design. Layer two is the one reading prose the contributor wrote, so a prompt injection that says "report this as safe" is addressing the layer with no vote, and the deterministic layer flags that sentence as a finding in its own right. A regex has nothing to persuade.

A review that did not happen must not look like a review that found nothing. If the diff fetch returns zero bytes, the job fails and the comment says, in those words, that it reviewed nothing. The page notes the same failure mode elsewhere in this repo: a Cloudflare ruleset that silently stopped applying, dotfiles dropped from a build artifact without an error, and a link checker that crawled zero URLs for eleven weeks behind a config typo and reported green every time.

The rule: when a job must process hostile input, decide first what it is allowed to do with it, then put every credential and every decision somewhere that input cannot reach. Here that means data instead of code, the verdict in a script instead of a model, and the write token in a different job.

== Three tokens, three roles
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: GITHUB_TOKEN | Automatic and scoped, usable for most repo operations | Its commits do not trigger downstream workflows | A deliberate anti-loop measure
col: csoh-ci App token | For bot commits that must trigger another workflow | Used when update-news opens a PR that needs checks
col: Personal Access Token | CSOH_PAT only approves that update-news PR | Needs workflow scope to push workflow files | Give it that scope from day one
If your bot's commits are not triggering CI, you are using GITHUB_TOKEN and need an App token or a PAT.
notes:
Every workflow run gets an automatic, scoped GITHUB_TOKEN, usable for most repo operations, but commits made with it do not trigger downstream workflows. That's a deliberate anti-loop measure. CSOH uses an installation token from its csoh-ci GitHub App when it wants a bot-pushed commit to trigger another workflow, for example when update-news opens a PR that needs validate-html and check-broken-links to run on it. A PAT, CSOH_PAT, only approves that PR.

PATs carry their own trap, which bit CSOH in late April 2026. GitHub blocks any PAT push that touches .github/workflows/ unless the PAT carries the workflow scope, a guardrail that stops a leaked PAT from rewriting CI to deploy malicious code. The catch is that the check runs against every commit in the push range, not just the bot's own commit. So once main has any workflow file changes, every later bot-driven force-push of a derived branch fails until the PAT has workflow scope.

The lesson: when you create your PAT, give it workflow scope from day one. Otherwise, the day you edit a workflow file, your bots break.

== Concurrency: queue or cancel?
layout: compare
section: anatomy | concepts
col: Queue: cancel-in-progress: false | For runs you can't lose, such as auto-PRs and scheduled content | Triggers stack and run one after another | CSOH: normalize-urls, update-news
col: Cancel: cancel-in-progress: true | For read-only checks per branch and idempotent deploys | Push a fix, the old run dies | CSOH: site-update-deploy, deploy, per-PR checks
With cancel-in-progress: true, a group that includes ${{ github.ref }} lets runs on one PR or branch cancel each other, while different PRs run in parallel.
notes:
The concurrency block in validate-html.yml names a group made of validate-html and the git ref, with cancel-in-progress set to true. Runs on the same ref, meaning the same PR or branch, cancel each other: push a fix, and the in-flight validation stops and a fresh one starts on the new commit. Different PRs run in parallel because the group includes the ref.

Mixing up queue and cancel causes either lost runs or lost time. Queue things you can't lose: auto-PR creators, scheduled content jobs, manual deploys. Cancel read-only checks per branch, and idempotent deploys where the latest push is the one you want shipped.

In CSOH's split, site-update-deploy cancels old runs in the deploy-pipeline group: housekeeping is idempotent, and cancelling avoids the "stale SRI commit wins the race" failure mode. normalize-urls shares that lock so the two never collide on the same commit-push sequence, but stays queued, because losing a URL-normalization sweep would be silent and surprising. deploy has its own group and cancels, since only the latest build needs to ship. update-news has its own group and queues; it opens a PR on its own branch and doesn't push to main. Per-PR checks use a per-ref group and cancel.

The mistake CSOH made: at first, deploy had no concurrency at all. Two news-update commits within a minute spawned two simultaneous deploys that interleaved their commits and corrupted the deployed site. Any concurrency group fixed the corruption; getting the split right took a few iterations.

== Step outputs drive conditionals
layout: code
section: concepts
A step can publish small key/value outputs that later steps in the same job read. Here, Deploy runs only when detect reports a change.
```yaml
- name: Did anything change?
  id: detect
  run: |
    if git diff --quiet; then
      echo "changed=false" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
    else
      echo "changed=true" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
    fi

- name: Deploy
  if: steps.detect.outputs.changed == 'true'
  run: ./deploy.sh
```
notes:
Steps can publish small key/value outputs that later steps in the same job can read. In this example the detect step checks whether git diff finds a change and writes changed=true or changed=false to the step's output. The Deploy step's if: condition reads that output and runs only when it is true.

The page says this is how CSOH's deploy job decides whether to run the publish step at all: if no SRI hashes, no sitemap, no previews and no HTML changed, the whole deploy is skipped.

== More concepts that bite
layout: table
section: concepts
row: Concept | What to know
row: git push from a runner | If main moved, rebase on the latest main and retry
row: if: always() | Runs even after a failure, handy for uploading a debug log
row: if: failure() | Runs only because something failed, for "your build broke" comments
row: Artifacts | Per-run, downloadable storage for reports; retention-days: 30 bounds it
row: Secrets | Masked as *** in run logs; never echo one to debug it
notes:
Even with concurrency groups, a push from a runner can race other workflows in different groups, or humans. The safer pattern is git push, and if that is rejected because main moved, git pull --rebase origin main and push again. CSOH uses this in every commit-and-push step in site-update-deploy.yml.

By default, a step skips if any earlier step failed. if: always() forces it to run anyway. if: failure() runs it only because something earlier failed. CSOH uses both extensively in its PR-check workflows.

actions/upload-artifact stuffs files into per-run storage that you can download from the run page. The page calls it critical for any step that produces a report: CSOH uses it for the safety-scan output, the link-rot crawl and the HTML validator log.

Anything in secrets.X is automatically masked in run logs, even if you wrote the workflow yourself. To see a secret, you have to use it. Never put secrets in the YAML directly; always reference them by name.

== Security baseline: pins, permissions and secrets
layout: checklist
section: security
check: Pin every Action to a full commit SHA, and make the repo enforce it
check: Set explicit permissions: on every workflow, defaulting to contents: read
check: Avoid pull_request_target unless you fully understand it
check: Don't echo or log secrets, or write them to artifacts
check: Use OIDC for cloud credentials when you can
Whatever you build, do these from day one.
notes:
Pin every Action to a full commit SHA, never @v3 or @main. Tags and branches can be moved silently by a compromised maintainer; SHAs cannot. Pinning by convention decays, because the enforcement mechanism is a reviewer noticing a tag in a diff at 5pm. So turn on GitHub's repository policy that requires actions to be pinned to a full-length commit SHA, sha_pinning_required, which this repo has enabled and which rejects an unpinned reference at run time. Use ratchet or Dependabot to keep the SHAs current, and leave the version in a trailing comment so the pin stays readable.

Set explicit permissions on every workflow. Default to contents: read, add only what you need, and remember the block only governs the ambient GITHUB_TOKEN.

pull_request_target runs in the context of the base branch with full secrets, on code from a fork. The breach kill chains page covers an attack pattern that exploited this on a major open source project.

Don't echo secrets, don't log them, and don't write them to artifacts, even in a debug step you'll remove later.

AWS, GCP and Azure all support short-lived federated tokens via OIDC, with no long-lived access keys to leak. That is how CSOH deploys: every publish job federates a short-lived token to its cloud, so there is not a single static cloud key in the repo.

== Security baseline: trust and credentials
layout: checklist
section: security
check: Pass github.event values through env:, not inline ${{ }} in run blocks
check: Pin cloud OIDC trust to the full subject, not just the repository
check: Never allowlist a bare interpreter for an agent, bot or plugin
check: Set persist-credentials: false unless a later step pushes
check: Verify the control where it lives, not only where you declared it
notes:
Treat github.event values as untrusted input when interpolating into shell. PR titles, branch names and commit messages can contain attacker-controlled strings, so use ${VAR} from env: instead of inline expressions in run blocks. The site's Semgrep rules guide builds and tests a rule that finds this pattern in your workflows.

A condition on the repository alone is what everyone writes first, and it trusts every workflow in the repo on every branch. Require the subject too, and let the GitHub Environment's deployment-branch rule carry the branch restriction, so there is one place to change rather than three.

python3, node, perl, sh and bash all take a -c flag, so allowlisting one of them is allowlisting everything and silently voids the rest of the list. Allowlist a checked-in script path instead. The page calls this non-negotiable in any job that reads web pages, issue text or feeds.

By default checkout stores the token it used in .git/config, where any step can read it back out as a file for the rest of the job. When the steps that need the token are passed it explicitly, the copy on disk is pure downside.

Infrastructure as code tells you what you asked for; it does not prove the edge is doing it. CSOH's deploy pipeline re-derives every published asset's SRI hash from what the CDN serves and compares the live security headers against the repo's Cloudflare Terraform. A forgotten apply, a dashboard edit, or a resource that silently stopped reconciling fails the deploy instead of sitting undetected in production.

== Read the repo in this order
layout: table
section: using-our-repo
row: Read | For
row: validate-html.yml, first | The trigger, step, output and annotation shape
row: The two validators | PR comments, caching, the read-only check shape
row: The two auto-PR workflows | Scheduling, auto-approve, conditional auto-merge
row: deploy.yml | OIDC to each cloud; build once, publish everywhere
row: site-update-deploy.yml, last | Step outputs, conditional steps, commits back to the repo
The validators are check-broken-links.yml and check-url-safety.yml. The auto-PR workflows are update-news.yml and normalize-urls.yml.
notes:
The page's point is that CSOH's repo is a working, in-production reference, and it suggests a reading order.

Start with the smallest, validate-html.yml: short and single-purpose, it runs an HTML validator on PRs and posts a comment. Then the validators, check-broken-links.yml and check-url-safety.yml, for PR-comment patterns, caching and the read-only check shape. Then the auto-PR ones, update-news.yml and normalize-urls.yml, for scheduling, the two-PAT auto-approve dance and conditional auto-merge. Then deploy.yml, the OIDC-token-to-cloud-credential exchange three times, once per cloud, plus build once, publish everywhere. Finally the big one, site-update-deploy.yml, for step outputs, conditional steps, committing back to the repo and chained workflows via push events.

Every workflow file in the repo is heavily commented: every non-obvious line has a 1-3 line explanation aimed at someone who has never written GitHub Actions before. Fork the repo, gut the content, keep the workflow scaffolding, and you can have a working static-site CI/CD in an hour. The page asks you to send a link in the Friday Zoom if you do.

== Why does validate-html.yml filter no paths on pull_request?
layout: question
section: workflows-tour
validate-html.yml is a required status check on main. Think about a Python-only PR, and what happens if a paths: filter keeps the workflow from starting.
notes:
This question comes from the tour of validate-html.yml. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== A filtered-out required check never reports
layout: answer
section: workflows-tour
A filtered-out workflow never starts, so it produces no check run at all. A required check that never reports blocks the PR forever, with nothing red to click. The filtering moved into a step that gates the expensive work.
notes:
A filtered-out workflow never starts, so it produces no check run at all: not skipped, not neutral, nothing. A required check that never reports blocks the pull request forever, with nothing red to click on. The page calls it a trap that is invisible until it bites.

So validate-html.yml's pull_request trigger carries no paths: filter. The path filtering moved one level down into a "Detect HTML changes" step that gates the expensive work, so a Python-only PR still gets a green check in seconds having done nothing. check-url-safety.yml, the other required check that used to be filtered, has the same shape.

== Why does every deploy re-upload the whole tree?
layout: question
section: concepts
Your deploy uses a tool such as rsync or aws s3 sync, which compares mtimes to decide what to upload. Think about what a fresh checkout does to them.
notes:
This question comes from the concepts section. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Checkout resets every mtime
layout: answer
section: concepts
A fresh checkout gives every file the same mtime: the moment it was checked out. Before deploying, rewind each tracked file's mtime to its last commit date, and have housekeeping write a file only when its content changes.
notes:
actions/checkout gives every file the same mtime, the moment it was checked out. Any tool that decides what to upload by comparing mtimes then re-uploads the entire tree on every run. The page says it took CSOH months to notice, because the deploy was just slow.

The fix is a short loop before the deploy step: walk every tracked file and rewind its mtime to the date of its last git commit. It pairs with a discipline rule for housekeeping scripts: only write a file when its content changes. Then the scripts give a fresh mtime to exactly the files that need it, and the deploy uploads only those. Together, the page says, they mean a no-op deploy does nothing. The loop is in site-update-deploy.yml.
